    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">52 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The Parisii are defeated by a Roman army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/titus-labienus">Titus Labienus</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> build <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutetia">Lutetia</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">508</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clovis-i">Clovis I</a> makes Paris his capital</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1163</span><span class="rf-text">Construction of the cathedral of Notre-Dame begins</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1348</span><span class="rf-text">The plague of 1348–1349 kills forty to fifty thousand Parisians, a quarter of the population</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1572</span><span class="rf-text">The St Bartholomew's Day massacre kills about three thousand Protestants in Paris</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1789</span><span class="rf-text">A crowd storms the Bastille on 14 July and Paris becomes the stage of the Revolution</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1871</span><span class="rf-text">The Commune is crushed in the Bloody Week</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1944</span><span class="rf-text">The city is liberated on 25 August after four years of German occupation</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/julius-caesar.jpg" alt="Julius Caesar" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Retrato_de_Julio_C%C3%A9sar_(26724093101)_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ángel M. Felicísimo from Mérida, España, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The oldest traces of human occupation in Paris date from about 8000 BCE, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesolithic">Mesolithic</a> period: archaeologists digging in the 15th arrondissement in 2008 found remains of a hunter-gatherer settlement. At Bercy, excavations in 1991 found fragments of three wooden canoes used by fishermen on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seine">Seine</a>, the oldest dated to 4800–4300 BCE, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> hatchets made in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, which show that the first Parisians already traded with other parts of Europe.</p><p>Between 250 and 225 BCE the Parisii, a Gallic tribe, settled on the banks of the Seine. One of the area's main north–south trade routes crossed the river on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ile-de-la-cite">Île de la Cité</a>, and the Parisii traded with river towns as far away as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberian Peninsula</a> and minted their own coins. They built an oppidum, a walled fort, at the beginning of the 2nd century BCE, but its site is disputed. It may have stood on the Île de la Cité, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> recorded meeting the leaders of the Parisii on an island in the Seine. Other historians point to the absence of early Gallic traces there and place it at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanterre">Nanterre</a>, where the remains of a large settlement were found in the 1980s. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a> called the settlement Lucotocia and Ptolemy Leucotecia. The meaning of the tribal name is debated: one proposal derives it from a Celtic root for 'cauldron'.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="growth">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Growth &amp; Great Ages</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/suger"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/suger.jpg" alt="Suger" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/suger">Suger</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Abbot_Suger.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Uploaded by Hedning to Swedish Wikipedia, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>After the Roman conquest the city lay mostly on the Left Bank, around a forum on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montagne-sainte-genevieve">Montagne Sainte-Geneviève</a>. It had an aqueduct sixteen kilometres long, baths and an amphitheatre for ten to fifteen thousand spectators, although its population was only six to eight thousand. It remained a medium-sized city, much smaller than Lugdunum (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyon</a>) or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sens">Agedincum</a> (Sens).</p><p>In the 12th century the Capetian kings, who held little more than Paris and its surroundings, built it up as the political, economic, religious and cultural capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. Work on Notre-Dame began in 1163, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suger">Suger</a> rebuilt the basilica of Saint-Denis in the style later called Gothic, and Philip II built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louvre">Louvre</a> fortress, new walls, stone bridges and the covered market of Les Halles. In 1328 the population was about 200,000, the largest of any city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. It reached 250,000 by 1500 and 400,000 in 1643, and was between 600,000 and 640,000 in 1789. Rebuilding from 1853 took it past a million, and its historic peak was 2.9 million in 1921. In the 16th century Paris printed more books than any other European city, and by the mid-19th century it was a financial centre second only to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rulers">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Who Ruled It</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/louis-xiv.jpg" alt="Louis XIV" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Louis_XIV_of_France.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Hyacinthe Rigaud, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> held Paris from 52 BCE. From 355 to 360 it was the base of Julian, the Caesar of the western provinces, whose soldiers proclaimed him Augustus in February 360, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valentinian-i">Valentinian I</a> and Gratian also wintered there. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clovis-i">Clovis I</a>, King of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a>, entered the city in 486 and made it his capital in 508. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-empire">Carolingians</a>, in power from 751, moved the Frankish capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aachen">Aachen</a>, and Paris regained its place under the Capetians, a dynasty founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hugh-capet">Hugh Capet</a> in 987.</p><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundred-years-war">Hundred Years' War</a> an Anglo-Burgundian army took the city on 28 May 1418, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-v-of-england">Henry V of England</a> held it from 1420, and the infant Henry VI was crowned king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> there in 1431. Charles VII returned in 1436. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-i-of-france">Francis I</a> made the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louvre">Louvre</a> his residence in 1534. After the Fronde, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> moved the court to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/versailles">Versailles</a> (in 1671 by one account, in 1682 by another), and the regent returned the government to Paris in 1715 before it went back to Versailles in 1722.</p><p>The Revolution gave Paris its first independent city council on 15 July 1789. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> replaced the elected mayor with a prefect of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seine">Seine</a> in 1800, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prefect">prefects</a> governed the city until Jacques Chirac became its first elected mayor since the Revolution on 25 March 1977. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii">Napoleon III</a> annexed the surrounding towns in 1860, and the Third Republic was proclaimed in Paris on 4 September 1870.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="life">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Life in the City</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/denis-diderot"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/denis-diderot.jpg" alt="Denis Diderot" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/denis-diderot">Denis Diderot</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Denis_Diderot_by_Louis-Michel_van_Loo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutetia">Lutetia</a> excavations have found <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amphora">amphorae</a> of Italian wine and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olive-oil">olive oil</a>, shellfish and the remains of garum. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> arrived in the middle of the 3rd century; according to tradition it was brought by Saint Denis, the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>, who was beheaded on the hill later called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montmartre">Montmartre</a>. The first recognised bishop, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victorinus">Victorinus</a>, is dated to 346, and by 392 the city had a cathedral.</p><p>Medieval Paris divided by function. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ile-de-la-cite">Île de la Cité</a> held the royal palace and the cathedral, the Left Bank the schools and monasteries, and the Right Bank the port, the market and the merchants, whose league, the Hanse parisienne, became a powerful force. The Left Bank was called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> Quarter because Latin was the language of teaching; some twenty thousand students lived there, and the teachers included Pierre Abelard, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-aquinas">Thomas Aquinas</a>, Roger Bacon and Bonaventure. Robert de Sorbon opened the college later named the Sorbonne in 1257. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-ix-of-france">Saint Louis</a> created the Provost of the Merchants and the first municipal council, of twenty-four members.</p><p>By the 1720s there were around 400 cafés, where writers and scholars met, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denis-diderot">Diderot</a> and d'Alembert published the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/encyclopedie">Encyclopédie</a> in 1751–52. Daniel Roche estimated that in 1700 between 150,000 and 200,000 people received poor relief, about a third of the population. In the 19th century large factories were built on the edges of the city, while the centre kept its artisans making clothing, watches, furniture, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porcelain">porcelain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leather">leather goods</a>.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Paris&#8221; cites where it covers Suburbs, Religion, Painting and sculpture, Theatre:</p><ul><li>Michael Dregni, Gypsy Jazz : In Search of Django Reinhardt and the Soul of Gypsy Swing: In Search of Django Reinhardt and the Soul of Gypsy Swing (Oxford University Press, 2008)</li><li>Haas Mroue, Frommer's Memorable Walks in Paris (John Wiley &amp; Sons, 2006)</li><li>Gillian Perry, Women Artists and the Parisian Avant-garde: Modernism and 'feminine Art' Art, 1900 to the Late 1920s (Manchester University Press, 1995)</li><li>Yvan Combeau, Histoire de Paris (Presses Universitaires de France, 2013)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="crises">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Sieges &amp; Disasters</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/vikings"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vikings.jpg" alt="Vikings" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Vikings</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Viking_attack_on_Gu%C3%A9rande,_from_a_Saint-Aubin_MS.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Abbey of Saint-Aubin, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In December 856 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Vikings</a> burned Paris, sparing a few churches for a ransom. Their siege of 885–886 failed against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seine">Seine</a> and the walls of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ile-de-la-cite">Île de la Cité</a>. The plague of 1348–1349 killed forty to fifty thousand Parisians, a quarter of the population, and there were at least 36 occasions of plague in the city between 1348 and 1480. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundred-years-war">Hundred Years' War</a> the city was occupied, and when the English left in 1436 many areas lay in ruins and 100,000 inhabitants, half the population, had gone.</p><p>On the night of 23–24 August 1572 the royal council decided to kill the Protestant leaders then in Paris for the wedding of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-iv-of-france">Henry of Navarre</a>, and Catholic mobs turned it into a general slaughter. About three thousand Protestants were killed in Paris and five to ten thousand elsewhere in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. In the Revolution, bands broke into the prisons in September 1792 and murdered priests, aristocrats and criminals. In the Terror, 16,594 people were tried by the revolutionary tribunal and guillotined.</p><p>The Prussian siege from 19 September 1870 brought cold and hunger, so that even the zoo's elephants were killed for food, and the city surrendered on 28 January 1871. The Commune that followed was retaken in the 'Bloody Week' of 21–28 May. Nearly 7,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communards">Communards</a> were killed in combat or shot afterwards, and about 10,000 were sentenced to prison or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">deportation</a>. On 16–17 July 1942 the French police arrested 12,884 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> on German orders, and most were sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oswiecim">Auschwitz</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/impressionism"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/impressionism.jpg" alt="Impressionism" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/impressionism">Impressionism</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Claude_Monet,_Impression,_soleil_levant.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Claude Monet, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Haussmann's rebuilding gave central Paris the street plan and the look it still has: buildings of the same height and design, faced in creamy white stone, along wide boulevards, with four large new parks and new sewers and water mains. The city was in turn the birthplace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/impressionism">Impressionism</a>, Fauvism and Cubism, the home of writers and artists of the 1920s in Montparnasse, and the setting of the intellectual life of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-germain-des-pres">Saint-Germain-des-Prés</a> after 1945. The first public projection of a motion picture was given at the Grand Café on 25 December 1895.</p><p>Paris is nicknamed the 'City of Light', which is attributed mainly to its leading role in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Age of Enlightenment</a>. The Eiffel Tower, built for the Exposition of 1889, was denounced at first in public letters by prominent cultural figures. Parts of the city along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seine">Seine</a> have been a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> since 1991. The fire of 15 April 2019 destroyed the spire and most of the roof of Notre-Dame, and the cathedral reopened on 7 December 2024.</p><p>Paris is today the capital and largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, with about two million inhabitants, fewer than its peak of 2.9 million in 1921, and the seat of the president and both houses of Parliament. Its metropolitan area has more than thirteen million people.</p></div><div class="profile-inv"><p class="profile-inv-k">Songs in history</p><p class="profile-inv-l"><a href="/songs/delphic-hymns">Delphic Hymns<span>c. 128 BCE</span></a><a href="/songs/te-deum">Te Deum<span>c. 380</span></a><a href="/songs/malbrough-sen-va-t-en-guerre">Malbrough s'en va-t-en guerre<span>1709</span></a><a href="/songs/o-come-o-come-emmanuel">O Come, O Come, Emmanuel<span>c. 1710</span></a><a href="/songs/frere-jacques">Frère Jacques<span>c. 1780</span></a><a href="/songs/au-clair-de-la-lune">Au clair de la lune<span>c. 1780</span></a><a href="/songs/gaudeamus-igitur">Gaudeamus igitur<span>1781</span></a><a href="/songs/ca-ira">Ça Ira<span>1790</span></a><a href="/songs/la-marseillaise">La Marseillaise<span>1792</span></a><a href="/songs/carmagnole">La Carmagnole<span>1792</span></a><a href="/songs/poland-is-not-yet-lost">Poland Is Not Yet Lost<span>1797</span></a><a href="/songs/the-wearing-of-the-green">The Wearing of the Green<span>1798</span></a><a href="/songs/le-temps-des-cerises">Le Temps des cerises<span>1866</span></a><a href="/songs/the-internationale">The Internationale<span>1871</span></a><a href="/songs/el-condor-pasa">El Cóndor Pasa<span>1913</span></a><a href="/songs/la-madelon">La Madelon<span>1914</span></a><a href="/songs/a-las-barricadas">A las Barricadas<span>1932</span></a><a href="/songs/imagine">Imagine<span>1971</span></a><a href="/songs/ya-rayah">Ya Rayah<span>1973</span></a></p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Paris grew from a settlement of the Gallic Parisii on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seine">Seine</a> into the Roman town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutetia">Lutetia</a> and, from 508, the capital of the Frankish kings and later of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. It was a centre of learning, the stage of the Revolution and the city remade by Haussmann.</em></p>

